This means the bug CANNOT be triggered through the recursive call when the child Sdb is already in the list.

But wait - what if the child Sdb is NOT the same as the one added to the list? What if there are multiple levels of indirection?

Actually, let me think about this differently. The `in_list` function checks `item == ns`. The `ns` variable is of type `SdbNs *`, but the list contains both `SdbNs *` and `Sdb *` pointers. When we call `in_list(list, child_sdb)`, it compares `child_sdb` (an `Sdb *`) with each element in the list. If an element happens to have the same pointer value (because it was added via `ls_append(list, ns->sdb)`), it matches.

So the recursive call is always short-circuited when the child Sdb was already processed. This means the bug must be triggered through a different path.

